Description: swaynag: opt-in keyboard control
 The bar shown before logging out, restarting or powering off could only
 be operated with a pointer: keys kept going to the focused window, so
 someone without a mouse could neither accept nor decline. The X11
 end-session dialog holds the keyboard, focuses its accept button and
 takes Return, space, Escape, Tab and the arrow keys.
 .
 swaynag gains a per-type keyboard-control=yes|no setting (also
 --keyboard-control, never abbreviated), off by default. When enabled the
 layer surface requests keyboard interactivity, so the bar takes the
 keyboard as soon as it is mapped, and keys are interpreted by keysym
 with the keymap and modifiers sent by the compositor. Exactly one button
 has focus, initially the first action button given (or the dismiss
 button). Tab and Shift+Tab (ISO_Left_Tab, or Tab with Shift) move it
 right or left and wrap; Right and Left do the same without wrapping.
 Return, KP_Enter and space press the focused button as a click would,
 once per physical press; Escape dismisses with status 0. With the
 details shown and overflowing, Up/Down scroll a line, Page Up/Page Down
 a page, Home/End to either end, within the limits of the scroll buttons.
 Navigation and scrolling keys repeat with the compositor's repeat rate
 and delay. Keys with Ctrl, Alt or Super are ignored, as are shifted keys
 other than Tab and the activation keys.
 .
 The focused button's border is drawn in the new focus-border color
 (--focus-border), which defaults to the resolved button-text color;
 with a zero button border a one pixel frame is drawn inside the button.
 The frame is drawn last, so the bottom border of the bar cannot cover it.
 Keys can change the bar faster than the compositor releases buffers, so
 a frame skipped for lack of a free buffer is drawn once one is released.
 Values other than yes and no are rejected with exit status 1. Without
 the setting nothing changes.
